module bindings.javascript.link_scheme_denylist_missing_data_scheme;

// A renderer or sanitizer that decides whether a link URL may be emitted by
// enumerating the schemes a browser executes documents from has four to
// enumerate, not three: `javascript:`, `vbscript:`, `file:` and `data:`. A
// `data:text/html;base64,...` URL is a document the browser parses in the
// embedding page's context -- `data:image/svg+xml` likewise, since SVG carries
// script and event-handler attributes -- so a scheme denylist that names
// javascript and vbscript but never mentions data returns exactly the execution
// it exists to refuse. Handling the data: scheme at all names it, in every form
// the documentation suggests: a quoted `'data'` entry in the refused list, a
// `data:image/` media-type allowlist for the one benign use, a `data|` branch in
// a scheme alternation, a `/^data:/i` pattern. So a module that enumerates
// browser-executable schemes and spells the data scheme nowhere has not
// considered it.
//
// `vbscript` is what makes the enumeration legible: nobody writes it in a URL by
// accident, and a module listing it beside `javascript` is enumerating schemes
// rather than languages. That is not quite enough on its own -- a
// syntax-highlighter grammar registry names both as language identifiers -- so
// the predicate also requires the scheme normalization a URL comparison needs
// (`toLowerCase`, since schemes are case-insensitive and `Data:` must compare
// equal to `data:`) and a URL-ish identifier in scope.
//
// Module scope rather than function scope: the scheme list is a module-level
// definition and the predicate consuming it is a handful of lines, so a
// function-scope query would see the membership test and none of the scheme
// names it has to read.
//
// Each scheme name is matched in a spelling that only appears where the name is
// data -- inside quotes, quote-then-colon, or as a `|`-delimited branch of a
// regex alternation -- and the data-scheme condition likewise, because a
// module-scope context carries the module's own source text, comments included,
// alongside its structured tokens. A bare `javascript` is satisfied by a comment
// saying which schemes a filter refuses, and a bare `data` by any prose about
// metadata or passing data between rules; the second is the damaging one, since
// a negative condition satisfied by prose declines the very module it is there
// to read.
binding linkSchemeDenylistMissingDataScheme {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "module"
    and node.context.language == "javascript"
    and containsAny(node.context.literal, ["'javascript'", "\"javascript\"", "'javascript:", "\"javascript:", "|javascript", "javascript|"])
    and containsAny(node.context.literal, ["'vbscript'", "\"vbscript\"", "'vbscript:", "\"vbscript:", "|vbscript", "vbscript|"])
    and node.context.call contains "toLowerCase"
    and containsAny(node.context.identifier, ["url", "uri", "href", "link"])
    and not containsAny(node.context.literal, ["data:", "'data'", "\"data\"", "|data", "data|"])
  emit issue code.HtmlSanitizerLogicReview at node
}
